/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getMode()
\brief Read the body's simulation mode.

\return The current simulation mode. Returns pdg.physicsBody_None for NoPhysics.

\see setMode()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setMode(number value)
\brief Set the body's simulation mode.

For ordinary bodies, switching out of dynamic mode clears forces. Static mode also clears linear and angular velocity. The absence value physicsBody_None cannot be assigned; remove the body from its owner instead. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation. Switching an owned body to dynamic mode cancels competing programmed movement/rotation tracks and rates while retaining size/appearance animation. A mounted Sprite body must remain kinematic until detached.

Mapped animation bodies instead route through Sprite::setAnimationPhysicsMode():
Dynamic releases rig tracking, Kinematic begins default 0.5-second recovery, and
Static is rejected. Recovery reports a dynamic body until it reaches its target.
This path preserves independent scheduled loads and rejects conflicting manual
or Part IK drives until explicitly released.

\param value One of \c pdg.physicsBody_Dynamic, \c pdg.physicsBody_Kinematic, or \c pdg.physicsBody_Static.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getMode()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getMass()
\brief Read the body's mass.

For a Sprite with an active explicit or generated animation physics rig, this is
the sum of registered component masses, including Kinematic components; unrelated
and detached Parts are excluded.

\return The current mass. Returns zero for NoPhysics.

\see setMass()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setMass(number value)
\brief Set the body's mass.

Scales inertia by newMass / oldMass, preserving a tuned inertia-to-mass ratio.
Linear/angular velocity is unchanged, so momentum changes. Invalid or overflowing
mass/inertia throws before changing state. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

For a Sprite with an active explicit or generated animation physics rig, scales
every registered component's mass/inertia while preserving their current
proportions. A component edit changes only that body and consequently the total.
The coordinating body adds no simulated mass.

\param value Finite positive mass in your application's mass units.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getMass()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getMomentOfInertia()
\brief Read the body's moment of inertia.

For an active Sprite rig, return the instantaneous inertia about its center of
mass: `sum(I_i + m_i * distanceSquared(r_i, COM))`. This is the inertia the current
pose would have if locked rigidly; it changes as articulated parts move. It counts
exactly the same attached components as getMass(), including Kinematic bodies.
The coordinating body's saved single-body inertia is not included.

\return The current moment of inertia in mass times distance squared. Returns
zero for NoPhysics.

\see setMomentOfInertia()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setMomentOfInertia(number value)
\brief Set the body's moment of inertia.

Changes inertia without changing angular velocity. Visual resizing does not recalculate inertia. On an active rig, set inertia on each component; the coordinating Sprite body rejects this setter. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

\param value Finite positive rotational inertia in mass times distance squared.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getMomentOfInertia()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getLinearDamping()
\brief Read the body's linear damping.

\return The current linear damping. Returns zero for NoPhysics. Units are inverse seconds.

\see setLinearDamping()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setLinearDamping(number value)
\brief Set the body's linear damping.

Damping slows velocity over time; it is separate from contact friction. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

\param value Finite nonnegative exponential damping rate per second. Zero disables damping.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getLinearDamping()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getAngularDamping()
\brief Read the body's angular damping.

\return The current angular damping. Returns zero for NoPhysics. Units are inverse seconds.

\see setAngularDamping()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setAngularDamping(number value)
\brief Set the body's angular damping.

Damping slows angular velocity over time; it is separate from contact friction. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

\param value Finite nonnegative exponential damping rate per second. Zero disables damping.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getAngularDamping()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getFriction()
\brief Read the body's contact friction.

\return The current contact friction. Returns zero for NoPhysics.

\see setFriction()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setFriction(number value)
\brief Set the body's contact friction.

Affects contacts, not free-motion damping. Sprite and Part collider shapes use this material unless they override it. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

\param value Finite nonnegative friction coefficient.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getFriction()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getRestitution()
\brief Read the body's restitution.

\return The current restitution. Returns zero for NoPhysics.

\see setRestitution()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setRestitution(number value)
\brief Set the body's restitution.

Controls bounce during contacts. It is independent of damping. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

\param value Finite coefficient from 0 (inelastic) through 1 (fully elastic).

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getRestitution()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getAngularVelocity()
\brief Read the body's angular velocity.

\return The current angular velocity. Returns zero for NoPhysics. Units are clockwise radians per second.

\see setAngularVelocity()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setAngularVelocity(number value)
\brief Set the body's angular velocity.

Static bodies retain zero angular velocity. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

A rigidly mounted Sprite's body rejects this operation; animate its mounting Part or detach it first.

\param value Finite angular velocity in radians per second; positive is clockwise.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getAngularVelocity()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getSpeed()
\brief Read the body's speed.

\return The current speed. Returns zero for NoPhysics. Units are distance per second.

\see setSpeed()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setSpeed(number value)
\brief Set the body's speed.

Preserves the current movement direction. A stationary body uses direction zero (positive x). Static bodies retain zero velocity. Invalid values throw on a present body. NoPhysics ignores the operation.

\param value Finite nonnegative speed in distance units per second.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getSpeed()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getSolver()
\brief Identify the active physics solver.

\return \c pdg.physicsSolver_None for NoPhysics, \c pdg.physicsSolver_Basic for the analytic integrator, or \c pdg.physicsSolver_Chipmunk for a layer-owned solver.

\see isPresent(), step()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getAngularMomentum()
\brief Read rotational momentum.

For an active Sprite rig, sum component spin and orbital momentum about the
assembly center of mass: `sum(I_i * omega_i + m_i * cross(r_i - COM, v_i - VCOM))`.
This includes attached Kinematic bodies, excludes the coordinating body and
unregistered/detached parts, and does not assume all parts rotate together.

\return Signed clockwise angular momentum in mass times distance squared per
second. For a single body, moment of inertia multiplied by angular velocity.

\see getMomentOfInertia(), getAngularVelocity(), applyAngularImpulse()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getMovementDirectionInRadians()
\brief Read the direction of linear velocity.

\return atan2(velocity.y, velocity.x), in radians. Zero points along positive x; positive angles turn clockwise.

\see setVelocityInRadians(), getSpeed()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::isPresent()
\brief Test whether this is a real physics body.

\return False for the shared NoPhysics object; true for a body created explicitly.

\see getMode(), isAttached()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::isAttached()
\brief Test whether an owner publishes this body's motion.

\return True while associated with a Sprite or Part. False after removal; a retained detached body remains usable.

\see step()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getState()
\brief Copy the current physical state.

Reads the active solver when one is attached. Changing the returned object does not change the body.

\return An owned object with the fields below.

\htmlonly[block]
<div class="shared-api-options">
\endhtmlonly

\anchor physics_body_state
\par Physics state
| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `x`, `y` | Center position in owning-layer distance units. |
| `rotation` | Clockwise angle in radians. |
| `velocityX`, `velocityY` | Linear velocity in distance units per second. |
| `angularVelocity` | Clockwise angular velocity in radians per second. |

\htmlonly[block]
</div>
\endhtmlonly

\see teleport(), setVelocity()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getVelocity()
\brief Read linear velocity.

\return A new Vector in owning-layer distance units per second.

\see setVelocity(), getSpeed()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setVelocity(Vector velocity)
\brief Set linear velocity.

Sets physical velocity immediately. Static bodies remain stationary; NoPhysics ignores the call.

A rigidly mounted Sprite's body rejects this operation; animate its mounting Part or detach it first.

\param velocity Velocity vector in owning-layer distance units per second.

\return This object, for chaining.

\par Example
\code{.js}
sprite.setupPhysicsBody().setVelocity(new pdg.Vector(20, 0));
\endcode

\see getVelocity(), applyImpulse()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setVelocity(number xPerSecond, number yPerSecond)
\brief Set linear velocity.

Sets physical velocity immediately. Static bodies remain stationary; NoPhysics ignores the call.

A rigidly mounted Sprite's body rejects this operation; animate its mounting Part or detach it first.

\param xPerSecond Finite horizontal velocity.
\param yPerSecond Finite vertical velocity.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getVelocity(), applyImpulse()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setVelocityInRadians(number speed, number direction)
\brief Set velocity from speed and direction.

Static bodies remain stationary. Invalid values throw; NoPhysics ignores the call.

\param speed Finite nonnegative speed in distance units per second.
\param direction Finite clockwise direction in radians, measured from positive x.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getMovementDirectionInRadians(), setVelocity()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::teleport(Point position, number radians)
\brief Move a body immediately.

Changes position and rotation while preserving velocity. An attached owner receives the new transform immediately. NoPhysics ignores the call.

A rigidly mounted Sprite's body rejects this operation; animate its mounting Part or detach it first.

\param position Finite center position in owning-layer coordinates.
\param radians Finite clockwise rotation in radians.

\return This object, for chaining.

\par Example
\code{.js}
body.teleport(new pdg.Point(100, 50), 0);
\endcode

\see getState()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::applyImpulse(Vector impulse)
\brief Change momentum immediately.

On a Sprite using setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(), routes the impulse to the
selected root component and returns the coordinating body for chaining. A world
application point keeps its lever arm relative to that root.

Linear velocity changes by impulse divided by mass. The overload with a world point also changes angular velocity using its lever arm. Only dynamic bodies respond; static, kinematic, and absent bodies ignore impulses.

\param impulse Finite momentum-change vector in owning-layer axes.

\return This object, for chaining.

\par Example
\code{.js}
body.applyImpulse(new pdg.Vector(4, 0));
body.applyImpulse(new pdg.Vector(0, 2), new pdg.Point(110, 50));
\endcode

\see applyForce(), applyAngularImpulse()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::applyImpulse(Vector impulse, Point worldPoint)
\brief Change momentum immediately.

On a Sprite using setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(), routes the impulse to the
selected root component and returns the coordinating body for chaining. A world
application point keeps its lever arm relative to that root.

Linear velocity changes by impulse divided by mass. The overload with a world point also changes angular velocity using its lever arm. Only dynamic bodies respond; static, kinematic, and absent bodies ignore impulses.

\param impulse Finite momentum-change vector in owning-layer axes.
\param worldPoint Finite application point in owning-layer coordinates. Omitting it applies at the center of mass.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see applyForce(), applyAngularImpulse()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::applyAngularImpulse(number impulse)
\brief Change angular momentum immediately.

On a Sprite using setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(), routes the impulse to the
selected root component and returns the coordinating body for chaining. A world
application point keeps its lever arm relative to that root.

Angular velocity changes by impulse divided by moment of inertia. Only dynamic bodies respond.

\param impulse Finite clockwise angular impulse in mass times distance squared per second.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see applyTorque(), getAngularMomentum()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::applyForce(Vector force, number durationSeconds, number delaySeconds)
\brief Apply a force for a finite duration.

Force integrates over time; it does not change velocity immediately. Use the point overload to add torque around the center of mass. JavaScript places worldPoint after durationSeconds and delaySeconds.

\htmlonly[block]
<div class="shared-api-options">
\endhtmlonly
\anchor physics_assembly_loads
\par Loads on an active Sprite rig
Finite and continuous forces/torques on `sprite.physics` act on the selected
physical root. The coordinating Sprite stays Kinematic and adds no simulated mass.
A new registration requires a Dynamic root; otherwise it returns zero. Driven
roots count as Dynamic, and these loads coexist with their controller.

The Sprite owns the returned IDs. Root reassignment or detachment routes existing
loads to the replacement root; cancel them through the same `sprite.physics`.
Component registrations use independent IDs and are not cancelled by clearing
Sprite loads. Delays and durations advance once per physics step. While the selected
root is Kinematic, existing loads age without exerting force and resume if it
becomes Dynamic. Snapshots preserve IDs and remaining durations. Loads surviving
rig disable continue on a Dynamic single body; a Kinematic/Static handoff clears them.

An off-center force captures its lever arm relative to the selected root at
submission. World force vectors and signed torques keep their world coordinates
across a Sprite flip. They are not reflected with the rig's pose and velocity.
\htmlonly[block]
</div>
\endhtmlonly

\param force Finite force vector in owning-layer axes, in mass times distance per second squared.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative active duration in seconds; zero applies no force.
\param delaySeconds Finite nonnegative delay before the force begins, in seconds.

\return A nonzero force ID for removeForce(), or \c pdg.physicsForce_None (0) for zero duration or a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const forceId = body.applyForce(new pdg.Vector(8, 0), 0.25);
// Off-center force, starting after 0.1 seconds:
body.applyForce(new pdg.Vector(0, 8), 0.25, 0.1, new pdg.Point(110, 50));
\endcode

\see applyImpulse(), removeForce()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::applyForce(Vector force, number durationSeconds, number delaySeconds, Point worldPoint)
\brief Apply a force for a finite duration.

Force integrates over time; it does not change velocity immediately. Use the point overload to add torque around the center of mass. JavaScript places worldPoint after durationSeconds and delaySeconds.

For active Sprite rigs, see \ref physics_assembly_loads.

\param force Finite force vector in owning-layer axes, in mass times distance per second squared.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative active duration in seconds; zero applies no force.
\param delaySeconds Finite nonnegative delay before the force begins, in seconds.
\param worldPoint Finite application point in owning-layer coordinates. The lever arm is captured when the force is submitted.

\return A nonzero force ID for removeForce(), or \c pdg.physicsForce_None (0) for zero duration or a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient.

\see applyImpulse(), removeForce()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::applyTorque(number torque, number durationSeconds, number delaySeconds)
\brief Apply torque for a finite duration.

Torque changes angular velocity according to moment of inertia. Only dynamic bodies respond.

For active Sprite rigs, see \ref physics_assembly_loads.

\param torque Finite clockwise torque in mass times distance squared per second squared.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative active duration in seconds; zero applies no force.
\param delaySeconds Finite nonnegative delay before the force begins, in seconds.

\return A nonzero force ID for removeForce(), or \c pdg.physicsForce_None (0) for zero duration or a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient.

\see applyAngularImpulse(), removeForce()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::addContinuousForce(Vector force)
\brief Apply continuous force.

Begins immediately and continues until removed, cleared, or an ordinary body leaves dynamic mode. Assembly loads follow the separate rules below.

For active Sprite rigs, see \ref physics_assembly_loads.

\param force Finite force vector in owning-layer axes, in mass times distance per second squared.

\return A nonzero force ID, or physicsForce_None (0) for a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const id = body.addContinuousForce(new pdg.Vector(0, 9.8));
// Later: body.removeForce(id);
\endcode

\see removeForce(), stopAllForces()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::addContinuousTorque(number torque)
\brief Apply continuous torque.

Begins immediately and continues until removed, cleared, or an ordinary body leaves dynamic mode. Assembly loads follow the separate rules below.

For active Sprite rigs, see \ref physics_assembly_loads.

\param torque Finite clockwise torque in mass times distance squared per second squared.

\return A nonzero force ID, or physicsForce_None (0) for a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const id = body.addContinuousTorque(2);
// Later: body.removeForce(id);
\endcode

\see removeForce(), stopAllForces()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::removeForce(uint id)
\brief Cancel a force or torque registration.

Works for pending, finite, and continuous loads. Removing an expired or unknown ID is harmless.

For active Sprite rigs, see \ref physics_assembly_loads.

\param id The ID returned when the force or torque was registered.

\return True if a pending/active load was removed; false otherwise.

\see applyForce(), applyTorque(), stopAllForces()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::stopAllForces()
\brief Clear active and delayed forces, torques and the physical drive.

Current linear and angular velocity are preserved. Use stopMoving() and stopSpinning() to clear those separately.

For active Sprite rigs, see \ref physics_assembly_loads.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see removeForce(), stopMoving(), stopSpinning()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::stopMoving()
\brief Clear linear velocity.

Does not clear forces; an active force can accelerate the body again on the next step.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see stopAllForces(), setVelocity()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::stopSpinning()
\brief Clear angular velocity.

Does not clear torque; an active torque can accelerate rotation again on the next step.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see stopAllForces(), setAngularVelocity()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::step(number deltaSeconds)
\brief Advance a detached body with the basic solver.

Integrates motion and load lifetimes. Throws if a Chipmunk solver owns the body or the time is invalid.

\param deltaSeconds Finite nonnegative elapsed time in seconds.

\return No value.

\note Sprite and Part owners advance attached bodies automatically. Call step() yourself only after detaching a retained basic body.

\see isAttached(), getSolver()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::setDriveTarget(Point position, number radians, number maxForce, number maxTorque, number frequency = 4, number dampingRatio = 1, int direction = rotationDirection_Shortest)
\brief Drive a dynamic body toward a position and orientation using bounded force and torque.

Installs or replaces an active spring/damper target. The operation preserves the
current position and velocity. Each physics step adds a drive contribution to
other applied loads; collisions and constraints still determine the solved pose.
Repeated successful manual setDriveTarget() calls replace the same manual target; the last call wins. Invalid updates preserve the previous target. A blocked or force-limited body may never reach its target. The drive stays enabled
until clearDrive(), stopAllForces(), or a change to kinematic/static mode.

\param position Target in owning-layer coordinates; a detached body uses its world frame.
\param radians Target orientation in radians, positive clockwise.
\param maxForce Finite nonnegative limit on the magnitude of the linear drive force, in mass units times distance units per second squared. Zero disables translation actuation.
\param maxTorque Finite nonnegative angular drive torque limit, in inertia units times radians per second squared. Zero disables angular actuation.
\param frequency Finite positive response frequency in cycles per second; default 4. Larger values request faster correction, subject to the force limits.
\param dampingRatio Finite nonnegative damping ratio; default 1 requests critical damping in the unconstrained linear model. Zero permits oscillation.
\param direction Integer rotationDirection constant; default rotationDirection_Shortest. Clockwise/CounterClockwise select a signed route at assignment. AsSpecified retains the unwrapped target, including full turns.

\return This PhysicsBody. NoPhysics returns the shared singleton without storing a target.

\note Requires a dynamic body. Invalid numeric values, unsupported direction
constants, or competing rigid-mount ownership throw before changing the target.
Limits bound only the drive contribution; independent impulses, forces, gravity
and constraint responses can produce additional motion. The basic solver uses
bounded spring/damper integration, while Chipmunk applies the drive before its
shared contact/constraint solve. Numerical trajectories need not match exactly.

\code
const body = sprite.setupPhysicsBody(2, 4);
body.setDriveTarget(new pdg.Point(120, 80), 0.5, 40, 12, 3, 1,
                    pdg.rotationDirection_Shortest);
// The Sprite/Part world advances its body. Query whether the goal is still blocked:
const remaining = body.getDriveState().positionError;
\endcode

\note Rig tracking and Part IK controllers reserve their participating drives. Release rig tracking with Sprite::setAnimationPhysicsMode(animationPhysics_Dynamic). Call Part::clearIKTarget() to release a Part IK chain
before replacing or clearing such a drive, stopping all forces, or changing away
from dynamic mode. Independent impulses and forces remain available.

\see getDriveState(), clearDrive(), setMode()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::clearDrive()
\brief Remove the active physical drive.

Preserves current position, linear/angular velocity and separately registered
forces/torques. Calling when no drive exists is harmless.

\return This PhysicsBody, including NoPhysics.
\see setDriveTarget(), stopAllForces()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::isDriveEnabled()
\brief Test whether a physical drive is enabled.

\return True while a drive is installed, including when its target is blocked
or already reached. False for NoPhysics.
\see getDriveState(), clearDrive()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::PhysicsBody::getDriveState()
\brief Copy the drive target, last actuation and current target error.

Returns an independent snapshot. When disabled, enabled is false and numeric
fields are zero. Reading never creates a body or emits a NoPhysics warning.

\htmlonly
<div class="shared-api-options" id="physics-drive-state">
<strong>Drive snapshot fields</strong>
<table>
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>enabled</td><td>Whether a drive is installed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>x, y, rotation</td><td>World/layer target position and resolved unwrapped angle in radians.</td></tr>
<tr><td>maxForce, maxTorque</td><td>Limits for the drive contribution.</td></tr>
<tr><td>frequency, dampingRatio</td><td>Response frequency in cycles/second and dimensionless damping ratio.</td></tr>
<tr><td>forceX, forceY, torque</td><td>Most recent internal integration step's drive contribution, excluding independent loads. Zero before the first step after target assignment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>positionError, rotationError</td><td>Current distance to the target and signed remaining unwrapped angle. These describe the actual body, including contact blocking.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
\endhtmlonly

\return A plain object with the fields above.
\see setDriveTarget(), isDriveEnabled()
*/
